{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/629132-the-wild-pantheon-totemic-version-2","id":629132,"title":"The Wild Pantheon (Totemic version 2)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-16T15:53:00-07:00","id":629132,"title":"The Wild Pantheon (Totemic version 2)","content":"Okay, so I tried this a while ago with my Totemic Pantheon and it turned out to be this whole, big thing that got a little complicated and lost momentum. Thanks to looking up the wiki, however, I found a much simpler way to explain a pantheon than writing up its entire detail in multiple segments. This is my second attempt at writing up a pantheon that revolves around the worship of animals, as opposed to human figures. So here we go!<br /> <br /> -----------------------------------------<br /> <br /> The Wild Pantheon revolves around the worship and veneration of the natural environment, and claims that industry and modern conviences have spoiled humanity and brought ruin to the planet. Like the Celestial Beurocracy, there are a staggering number of deities who comprise the pantheon. Some claim there is a god or goddess for every species of animal in the world. However, only a handful in the pantheon are actually powerful enough to create scions of their own. Instead of representing one individual species, these deities represent whole ecosystems or dietary habits, giving them more power to draw upon.<br /> <br /> Members of the pantheon attempt to remain in the forms of animals as much as possible, only taking on human form when it is necessary to accomplish their goals. In their natural environment, the closest they come is usually some form of hybridized beastman, possessing the same capacity as human movements, but still remaining bestial enough for them to feel comfortable. Even when in human form, however, they often decorate their clothing or skin with the image of a beast in some way. A constant reminder of their true selves amidst human civilization.<br /> <br /> The specific Purview of the pantheon is called Totem, a form of magic that allows them to channel power through artistic mediums such as carvings, tattoos, dances, clothing or body language. Their overworld is called The Animal Kingdom, which is surrounded and besieged by Prestigious, the greater titan of luxury and sophistication. Their underworld is called the Wildlands, a place where the souls of the dead await their rebirth into the next life.<br /> <br /> The followers of this pantheon believe in a process of reincarnation known as the Circle of Life. This cycle of reincarnation begins at the lowest point on the food chain and gradually progresses in rank until reaching the top, at which point it loops back around to the bottom. The exact time it takes to be reborn into the next life can vary from one individual to the next, but it rarely takes longer than 40 days. The purpose of the Circle of Life is to reafirm that all are equal in nature's eyes by allowing the soul to experience every tier in the natural order.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>History:</b> The worship of animals and nature is one of the most ancient religions in human history, predating even the concept of human figured gods and goddesses. But as humanity became more powerful and convinced itself they were superior, the worship of these powers began to dwindle. It is now only a mere shadow of its former glory, with those who continue to adhere to such teachings being seen as paupers and loonies.<br /> <br /> But that changed with the return of the Titans and the subsequent chaos they brought forth. Although significantly weakened in power, the deities of the animal kingdom retaliated to defend their realm from these monsters and safeguard the lands. This brought them back into the eye of the public, who started to realize that maybe their followers weren't completely deranged after all.<br /> <br /> Since the Titans reappeared, the pantheon has been slowly rebuilding its power in the mortal world. More and more people are coming to pay their respects, or to offer their service in some way. While ironically the Titan invasion is responsible for saving them from becoming a dead faith, they threaten the pantheon just as much as obscurity and so they continue to fight back.<br /> <br /> ---------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Nature:</b> To those outside the pantheon or religion, it is easy to mistake the Wild Pantheon for being savages. But the truth is that they do adhere to a sense of balance and order. The problem is that humanity has lived outside of this order for so long that they are no longer capable of recognizing it as such. They look at nature and see only chaos and barbarism, not the delicate ecosystem which thrives there.<br /> <br /> At its core, the pantheon is dedicated to the preservation of nature and its subjects. All of its subjects, not just the most privilaged. All creatures are susceptible to the Circle of Life, whether they be the helpless mouse or the mighty tiger. The actions and judgements of the pantheon may seem harsh to newcomers, but they are always fair in the grand scheme of things.<br /> <br /> The call of the wild is bred into their very beings. Members of this pantheon grow agitated if they are seperated from their wilderness for extended periods of time. They have even been known to turn violent if they are forcefully detained. Even Scions of this pantheon will find that they grow frustrated by modern society and the conviences that it offers them. Those who choose to live in a particular given area, tend to live on the outskirts of society, along nature trails or log cabins. But there are also those who take up the life of a wanderer, camping in the great outdoors and surviving off the fat of the land.<br /> <br /> ---------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Relationship:</b> The Animal Kingdom realized that if it did not want to be abandoned by humanity again, it had to adjust its approach to dealing with society. They can't just send a lion into a town without having it killed or captured by terrified villagers. If they were going to deal with society, they had to blend in with it, at least for a short while.<br /> <br /> When members of the pantheon walk among humans, they always keep the spirit of nature alive in their hearts. While they may look human, they are more in touch with their wild side and prone to emotions or instincts rather than higher forms of reasoning. Those who hide among low born society come across as rebels and dissidents, but even those who hide among the upper class are viewed by their peers as being eccentric or unorthadox in their mannerisms.<br /> <br /> Regardless of what role they take, The Animal Kingdom does not like being seperated from the wild for any great lengths of time. More often than not, their stay lasts only as long as is necessary to conceive a Scion or to pass on their messages to their worshipers, after which time, they return to animal form and vanish into the wilderness.<br /> <br /> -------------------------------<br /> <br /> <b>Members:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Predator:</b> Overgod of all carnivorous beasts, also the husband of Prey. He is one of the trinity powers that governs the pantheon<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Prey:</b> Overgoddess of all herbivore beasts, also the wife of Predator. She is one of the trinity powers that governs the pantheon<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Forager:</b> Overgod of omnivorous beasts and the child of Predator and Prey. He is one of the trinity powers that governs the pantheon. Unlike his Scion relatives, he was born a full god, thanks to his full blood parents.<br /> <br /> <br /> -----------------------------<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Secondary Members</b><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Aqua:</b> Goddess of underwater ecosystems. On the surface, she appears beautiful and captivating, but deep down, she can be mysterious and terrifying. <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Primal:</b> God of jungle ecosystems. He has been described as a hard ass rebel who only submits to shows of brute force.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Dune:</b> God of desert ecosystems and the brother of Tundra. He is a harsh and unforgiving god, prone to inflicting death and madness for even the tiniest mistakes within his realm.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Tundra:</b> Goddess of arctic ecosystems and the sister of Dune. Although her realm contains traces of beauty, she is every bit as merciless as her brother.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Burrow:</b> God of subterranean ecosystems. Totally blind, he navigates his surroundings by use of his extremely acute senses of touch and smell.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Forest:</b> Goddess of woodland ecosystems. She is perhaps one of the more graceful and serene members of the pantheon.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Aerial:</b> Goddess of mountainous ecosystems and sibling to Canopy. She demands caution and grace to survive traversing her realm.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Marsh:</b> God of wetland ecosystems. Because many of his servants are amphibious, he is regarded as a cousin to Aqua.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Canopy:</b> God of rain forest ecosystems and sibling to Forest. Although dangerous to the inexperienced, his realm holds great beauty and beneficial ingredients.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Pasture:</b> God of grassland ecosystems. His realm is simple, yet functional.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Barren:</b> God of the underworld and inhospitable climates. He brings forth death and decay to keep the circle of life in motion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name